 17592186045417 true] [17592186424278 1071 "Consider the function $y={x}^{(2x+1)}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424278 1072 #uuid "09ef39a3-aa47-5850-a2b1-1c368b15a626" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424278 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2x+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2x+1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{2x+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2x+1}(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x+1}(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}\\cdot x(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}(2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1.$" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424277 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424279 1069 "q-138851" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424279 1071 "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424279 1072 #uuid "82f90d91-406e-5d3c-bce9-9c8235648ab0" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424279 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(2x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(2x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424275 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424280 1069 "q-138807" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424280 1071 "Consider the function $y=(3{x}^{2})^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(3{x}^{2})^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424280 1072 #uuid "bc4aa46c-af83-51d5-a531-dab6715c6436" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424280 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(3{x}^{2})^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3{x}^{2})^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot 6x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(3{x}^{2})^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(3{x}^{2})^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2.$" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424281 1058 #edb/ref 17592186424282 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424281 1062 #uuid "4bf48ceb-23da-56bd-b48d-6f977a912d9b" 13194139534527 17592186045417 true] [17592186424281 1063 #edb/re